\section{Refreshing a Bimorphic Cache After a Method Change}

A dispatch cache can preserve ordinary lookup while retaining
two hot cases. In this teaching runtime each call site has a
fixed interface slot, at most two entries, and a global method
epoch. A change affecting dispatch increments the epoch before
further calls. Assume execution is single-threaded, epochs do
not wrap, and the receiver is nonnull. The cache key is the
receiver's exact class, and an entry contains the target plus
any adjustment needed for the receiver. Use a least-recently-used
policy purely to select which performance hint to forget.
The output is the call's result, as in ordinary dispatch;
the cache and its statistics are not part of that result.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input receiver $v$, arguments $A$, slot $s$, site cache $C$
  \Output result of dispatch on the supplied receiver
  \If{$C.epoch\ne\mathit{methodEpoch}$}
    \State clear $C.entries$ \Comment{Old targets are no longer valid}
    \State $C.epoch\gets\mathit{methodEpoch}$
  \EndIf
  \State $k\gets\Call{ExactClass}{v}$
  \State $t\gets C.entries[k]$
  \If{$t$ is missing}
    \State $t\gets\Call{LookupSlot}{k,s}$
    \State insert $(k,t)$, evicting the oldest use if full
  \EndIf
  \State mark $k$ as most recently used
  \State \Return $\Call{Invoke}{t,v,A}$
  \Comment{Use the receiver tested above}
\end{sudocode}

For the slot \texttt{weight}, let the methods for two shapes
$A$ and $B$ return $3$ and $5$ initially. At epoch $8$, calls
on receivers of classes $A,B,A$ return $3,5,3$; the first
two miss and the third hits. Update only $B$'s method to
return $7$, advancing the epoch to $9$. The next $B$ call
clears the cache, resolves the new target, and returns $7$.
The next $A$ call misses too, even though it still returns
$3$. That extra miss is the price of this coarse version
dependency, not evidence that $A$ changed.

\Cref{fig:visual-150} contrasts the two misses after the epoch
change: only one method acquired a new result, but both entries
were discarded.

